## Goal
See how the six samples in this project cluster: a principal component analysis of the
gene-level counts, with each sample coloured by its condition (untreated vs treated).

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| `counts_gene` | `01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper` (native) | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv` |
| `design` | `01_prepare_samplesheets` (native) | `01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv` |

## Method
All steps run in one Python script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`, in the `gars-bio` conda
environment (pandas, numpy, pydeseq2 0.5.4, scikit-learn 1.9.0, matplotlib 3.11.1), submitted
as a single batch job. Inputs are read only; nothing is written outside this directory.

1. Resolve both inputs at run time with `_system/resolve_artifact.py --type counts_gene` and
   `--type design`; fail if either does not resolve.
2. Load the counts matrix (genes x samples; drop the `gene_id`/`gene_name` annotation columns,
   index by `gene_id`). Load the design and check that its `sample_id` set equals the counts'
   sample columns exactly; fail on any mismatch. Order samples as in the design.
3. Round the length-scaled counts to integers (standard practice for tximport-scaled counts
   entering DESeq2) and drop genes with zero counts in every sample.
4. Variance-stabilising transform with pydeseq2: `DeseqDataSet(counts, metadata=design,
   design="~condition")`, then `dds.vst(use_design=False)` (blind to condition, so the
   transform does not use the labels being visualised). Take the VST matrix from
   `dds.layers["vst_counts"]`.
5. Select the 500 genes with the highest variance across samples of the VST values (all
   genes if fewer than 500 remain), the DESeq2 `plotPCA` default.
6. PCA with `sklearn.decomposition.PCA` on the selected genes, samples as observations,
   genes centred (not scaled), `n_components = min(n_samples, n_genes)` = up to 6.
7. Write the per-sample coordinates on every component, with `condition`, `group` and
   `replicate` from the design, to `results/pca_coordinates.csv`; write the variance explained
   (proportion and cumulative) per component to `results/pca_variance_explained.csv`.
8. Plot PC1 vs PC2 with matplotlib: one point per sample, coloured by `condition` (two
   colours, legend), each point labelled with its `sample_id`, axis labels carrying the
   percentage of variance explained. Save as `results/pca_pc1_pc2.png` (300 dpi) and
   `results/pca_pc1_pc2.pdf`.

